E = [
("02-01","Erasmus Hall in Flatbush produced two American icons","Brooklyn","1959","Barbra Streisand and Neil Diamond both sang in the chorus at Erasmus Hall High School on Flatbush Avenue, a short walk from Cortelyou Road. They barely knew each other then.\n\nErasmus Hall is the oldest secondary school in New York State, founded in 1786 when Flatbush was a Dutch farming village, and the original wooden academy building still stands in the courtyard of the Gothic quadrangle that grew up around it. For most of the twentieth century it was an enormous public high school serving a neighbourhood in constant demographic change, and its alumni list is correspondingly improbable.\n\nStreisand and Diamond overlapped in the choral club without any particular sense of each other. Both have described the connection as a footnote they only found interesting decades later, when they recorded 'You Don't Bring Me Flowers' as a duet in 1978 — a record that reached number one and was itself something of an accident, assembled after a radio programmer spliced their separate solo versions together and listeners demanded the real thing.\n\nThe school's cultural output extends well beyond them: Clive Davis, who would sign Bruce Springsteen and Whitney Houston, came through the same building, as did a long list of writers, athletes, and chess players.\n\nIt is a useful reminder of what a functioning neighbourhood school produced when the neighbourhood was dense, mixed, and the school was properly funded.",["They later recorded the duet 'You Don't Bring Me Flowers' in 1978","Clive Davis, who signed Springsteen and Whitney Houston, also went to Erasmus"],"crowd"),
("02-02","The Kings Theatre sat dark for 36 years","Brooklyn","1977","The Flatbush Avenue movie palace closed in 1977 and decayed with trees growing through the roof. A restoration reopened it in 2015 as one of Brooklyn's great music rooms.\n\nIt opened in 1929 as one of the five Loew's Wonder Theatres, built across New York and New Jersey in a French-baroque style calculated to be overwhelming. Ornamental plaster, a vast lobby, gilded detail, and somewhere in the region of three thousand seats — the point was to make an ordinary evening at the pictures feel like an event in a palace.\n\nThe economics that supported that model collapsed. Television, suburbanisation, and the fragmentation of moviegoing into small multiplex screens left enormous single-screen houses unviable, and Kings closed in 1977. It then sat empty for thirty-six years, taking on water, losing its roof in places, and becoming one of the most photographed ruins in the city.\n\nThe restoration was a serious piece of craft work. Surviving fragments of the 1929 plasterwork were used to cast replacements, and much of the interior detail people now see is a reconstruction from physical evidence rather than a guess.\n\nIt reopened in 2015 as a working concert hall roughly a mile from Cortelyou Road, which changed the touring map — artists who would previously have played Manhattan now have a reason to stop in Flatbush.",["Original 1929 plasterwork was recast from surviving fragments","It now seats roughly 3,000 for concerts a mile from Ditmas Park"],"crowd"),
("02-03","Biggie recorded his debut while still selling on the corner","Hip-Hop","1994","'Ready to Die' was cut while Christopher Wallace was living on St. James Place in Clinton Hill. Producers have described him writing nothing down, holding entire verses in his head.\n\nThe composition method is the detail other rappers have fixated on ever since. He would listen to a beat, sit with it, and then deliver a full verse from memory without ever putting pen to paper — a practice Jay-Z has said he adopted directly after watching it. It requires holding an enormous amount of structure in your head at once, and it tends to produce a more conversational rhythm than writing does, because the lines are built by ear rather than by eye.\n\nThe album's structure is deliberately autobiographical. The skits move through his actual upbringing in that part of Brooklyn, and the sequencing runs from childhood to the closing track's suicide, which was an unusually bleak way to end a debut that also contained radio singles.\n\nSean Combs pushed hard for those singles, and the tension between the record's commercial and street instincts is audible. It resolved in the album's favour: it worked on both counts.\n\nWallace was murdered in Los Angeles in March 1997, weeks before his second album was released. He was twenty-four.",["He never used a pen in the booth, a habit Jay-Z also adopted","The album's skits map his actual Brooklyn upbringing"],"mic"),
("02-04","The Beastie Boys started as a Brooklyn hardcore band","Punk","1981","Before rapping, the Beastie Boys played fast hardcore punk around New York. Their pivot to hip-hop began as a joke track that got better reactions than their punk set.\n\nThe first release was Polly Wog Stew, a hardcore EP, and the band operated inside the same downtown and outer-borough punk circuit as everyone else playing that music in the early eighties. Kate Schellenbach, later of Luscious Jackson, played drums.\n\nThe joke track was 'Cooky Puss', built around a prank phone call to a Carvel ice cream shop over a beat. It got played in clubs, it got a reaction the punk material was not getting, and the band followed the reaction. Rick Rubin, then producing out of an NYU dorm room, became the connection into hip-hop proper.\n\nThe transition was not smooth or particularly principled at the time. Licensed to Ill sold enormously and leaned on a frat-party persona the band spent much of the following decade repudiating, publicly and repeatedly.\n\nWhat is genuinely unusual is that they kept playing instruments. By Check Your Head they were performing as a band again, and the later records combine live playing, sampling, and rapping in a way almost nobody else attempted. The punk years turned out not to be a phase they left behind.",["Their first release was a hardcore EP, not a rap record","They kept playing instruments themselves long after going platinum"],"guitar"),
("02-05","Coney Island's boardwalk had its own sound","Brooklyn","1950","Amusement park organs, barkers and competing radios created a dense collage that New York songwriters mined for decades. The Tilyou family's Steeplechase organ was audible for blocks.\n\nBand organs were engineering solutions to a specific problem. Before amplification was practical outdoors, a fairground needed music loud enough to carry over crowds, machinery, and the ocean, and the answer was a large mechanical organ driven by pneumatics and paper rolls, voiced deliberately bright and reedy so it would cut through. They are not subtle instruments because subtlety was never the brief.\n\nStack several of those against barkers working the sideshows, competing radios, the Cyclone, and the crowd itself and you get a genuinely dense sonic environment — one that a lot of New York songwriting has drawn on, consciously or not, as shorthand for a particular kind of summer.\n\nThe area gained a second layer later. Brighton Beach, immediately east along the same boardwalk, became home to a large Soviet and post-Soviet Jewish community from the 1970s, and its restaurants and clubs brought Russian and Ukrainian popular music into the same stretch of coastline.\n\nBoth layers are still audible on a hot Saturday, which makes the boardwalk one of the few places in the city where you can hear a hundred years of immigration in about a fifteen-minute walk.",["Band organs were built to cut through crowd noise without amplification","Brighton Beach's Russian clubs later added a second musical layer nearby"],"crowd"),
("02-06","Bob Marley was born on this day","Reggae","1945","Born in rural Nine Mile, Jamaica, Marley moved to the Kingston slum of Trench Town as a teenager, where the Wailers formed around vocal-group harmony, not roots reggae.\n\nThe early Wailers were a vocal group in the American mould, closer to the Impressions than to anything the name now suggests. Marley, Peter Tosh, and Bunny Wailer sang three-part harmony over ska — music considerably faster and brighter than the roots reggae of the seventies — and they were coached by Joe Higgs, a local singer who taught them how to blend.\n\nThe tempo change came in stages. Ska slowed into rocksteady in the mid-sixties, and rocksteady slowed further into reggae, with the bass moving forward and the emphasis settling onto the offbeat. Marley's most famous work sits at the end of that process, not the start of it.\n\nHe had trained as a welder before committing to music, which is the sort of detail that gets lost in the iconography. Trench Town in that period was a government yard housing scheme, poor and violent, and music was one of a very small number of routes out.\n\nHe died of melanoma in 1981, at thirty-six. The cancer began in a toe and was initially misdiagnosed; by the time it was treated seriously it had spread.",["Their earliest records were ska, much faster than what he became known for","He studied welding before committing to music"],"mic"),
("02-07","Brooklyn's Fort Greene birthed a jazz-rap scene","Hip-Hop","1990","In the early nineties, Fort Greene brownstones hosted a circle of artists that fed groups like Digable Planets and later Black Star's Mos Def and Talib Kweli.\n\nThe neighbourhood had cheap-enough brownstones, proximity to Manhattan, and a concentration of Black artists, filmmakers, writers, and musicians who knew each other socially. Spike Lee's production company was based there. The scene was not organised around a venue or a label so much as around apartments, and that informality is part of why it produced work that sounds unhurried.\n\nMusically it pushed against what was dominant. The early nineties mainstream was moving toward harder drums and more aggressive posture; this circle went the other way, building tracks on jazz samples, live instrumentation, and a conversational delivery. Digable Planets are the clearest early example, and Black Star's 1998 debut is the fullest statement of the approach.\n\nTalib Kweli co-owned Nkiru Books, a Black bookstore in Park Slope, which tells you something about how the scene understood itself — as continuous with a longer intellectual tradition rather than as a genre exercise.\n\nFort Greene is now one of the more expensive neighbourhoods in Brooklyn, which is the usual ending. The conditions that made the scene possible were removed, in large part, by the neighbourhood's own success.",["Kweli co-owned Nkiru Books, a Park Slope Black bookstore","Black Star's debut leaned on live jazz samples rather than hard drums"],"turntable"),
("02-08","The click track was invented to sync film, not music","Film","1930","Early sound-film composers punched holes in the film itself to make audible clicks, letting orchestras lock to the picture. Recording studios borrowed it decades later.\n\nThe problem was precise. A composer scoring a scene needed the orchestra to hit a musical accent at an exact frame, and there was no way to feed a conductor a reliable tempo reference. The solution was mechanical: punch holes in the soundtrack area of the film at regular intervals so that, when run through the projector, they produced audible clicks in the musicians' headphones at a fixed rate derived from the frame count.\n\nMusicians disliked it. A conductor's tempo breathes, and locking an orchestra to a metronome removes exactly the flexibility that makes an ensemble expressive. The objection was correct and the technique won anyway, because synchronisation mattered more.\n\nRecording studios adopted it much later, and the incentive there was different — multitrack overdubbing requires that a part recorded on Tuesday line up with one recorded on Friday, which is impossible if the tempo drifts.\n\nEverything downstream of that follows: tape loops, drum machines, sequencers, quantisation, and the DAW grid that virtually all contemporary pop is built on. A device invented so a French horn could hit a door slam turned into the fundamental unit of modern record production.",["Musicians initially resisted it as mechanical","Today nearly every pop record is built on a grid descended from it"],"tape"),
("02-09","The Beatles played Ed Sullivan to 73 million people","Rock","1964","Broadcast from CBS Studio 50 in Manhattan, the performance was watched by roughly 40 percent of America. Crime rates that hour reportedly dipped.\n\nThe booking came out of an accident of timing. Sullivan had been at Heathrow in late 1963 and witnessed the crowd chaos around the band's return from Sweden, and he booked them on the strength of the scene rather than the music, which he had not particularly heard.\n\nThe scale of the audience was a function of a media environment that no longer exists. Three networks, one time slot, and no way to watch later meant a single broadcast could genuinely reach forty percent of a country at once. Nothing since has been able to concentrate attention that way, which is why the moment gets treated as a turning point rather than a television booking.\n\nThe studio had roughly seven hundred seats and received something in the region of fifty thousand ticket requests.\n\nThe often-repeated claim about crime dropping during the broadcast is anecdotal and has never been properly substantiated, but it survives because it captures something true about the concentration involved.\n\nThe practical consequence was immediate. Within months American labels were signing British guitar bands indiscriminately, and the balance of trade in popular music reversed for the better part of a decade.",["The studio received 50,000 ticket requests for 700 seats","Sullivan had booked them after seeing fan chaos at Heathrow"],"radio"),
("02-10","Dub was born by accident when a vocal track was left off","Reggae","1968","Engineer King Tubby pressed a version of a record with the vocals missing. Dancers loved it, so he began deliberately stripping tracks and drenching them in echo.\n\nThe context was competitive. Jamaican sound system operators fought for crowds, and an exclusive record nobody else could play was a genuine advantage. Cutting a one-off acetate with the vocal removed gave an MC room to talk over a familiar tune, which was initially a practical convenience rather than an artistic move.\n\nTubby — Osbourne Ruddock, an electronics repairman by trade — turned it into a compositional practice. He rewired mixing desks by hand, built his own filters, and used spring reverb and tape delay as performance instruments, dropping instruments in and out live while the mix was being cut. The mixing desk became something you played.\n\nWhat that produced was a form organised around absence. A dub mix is defined by what has been removed and by the space the removal leaves, with the drum and bass carrying the structure and fragments of the original surfacing and vanishing.\n\nThe logic travelled a long way. The extended remix, the twelve-inch dance mix, and the entire idea that a recording is raw material to be reworked rather than a fixed object all descend from this, and most electronic dance music is built on it.",["He rewired mixing desks by hand to create his effects","Dub's remix logic is the direct ancestor of electronic dance music"],"mixer"),
("02-11","Whitney Houston's mother sang backup for Aretha","Soul","1967","Cissy Houston led the Sweet Inspirations, who sang behind Aretha Franklin and Elvis. Whitney grew up in studios watching that work.\n\nThe Sweet Inspirations were among the most in-demand backing groups of the era, and their credits run through an enormous amount of the period's soul and pop. They sang behind Aretha Franklin at Atlantic during her defining run, worked with Elvis Presley in Las Vegas, and appeared on sessions across genres.\n\nThe family connections are remarkable in their density. Cissy Houston is Whitney's mother; Dionne Warwick is her niece, and therefore Whitney's cousin. The result is that Whitney Houston grew up inside professional sessions, watching how a vocal is constructed rather than being told about it.\n\nThat upbringing is audible in the technique. Her control of phrasing and dynamics came out of gospel and out of proximity to session-level craft, which is a different education from being a naturally gifted singer discovered late.\n\nBackground singers were, for decades, structurally underpaid and uncredited despite being on many of the records people remember, a point the documentary Twenty Feet from Stardom made forcefully.\n\nWhitney Houston died in 2012. Cissy Houston, who had spent a career making other people sound better, outlived her daughter by twelve years.",["Dionne Warwick is her cousin","Cissy also sang on Van Morrison's 'Brown Eyed Girl' sessions era material"],"mic"),
("02-12","Handel wrote 'Messiah' in 24 days","Classical","1741","Working almost without sleep, Handel completed the 259-page score in just over three weeks. A servant reported finding him weeping at his desk.\n\nThe speed is less miraculous than it sounds, and more impressive for a different reason. Handel, like most composers of the period, reused material freely — several movements adapt his own earlier Italian secular works — and he wrote in a highly systematised style where a great deal could be produced quickly by a fluent professional. What is remarkable is not that he wrote fast but that he wrote this fast and produced something that has not left the repertoire in nearly three centuries.\n\nHis circumstances mattered. Italian opera, the form he had built his career on, was collapsing commercially in London, and he was in serious financial difficulty. The English-language oratorio was a pivot toward a cheaper form with a broader audience and no staging costs.\n\nIt premiered in Dublin in 1742 as a charity performance, not in London. Demand was high enough that the organisers asked ladies to come without hooped skirts and gentlemen without swords so more people could be fitted into the hall.\n\nThe tradition of standing for the Hallelujah chorus is attributed to George II, though there is no contemporary evidence he was even present.",["It premiered in Dublin, not London","Audiences were asked to leave hoops and swords at home to fit more people in"],"strings"),
("02-13","The 'Loud War' shrank music's dynamic range","Production","1995","Mastering engineers began compressing records to sound louder than competitors on radio and CD changers. The side effect was flattened peaks and listening fatigue.\n\nThe mechanism is competitive and self-defeating. Louder registers as better on a first listen — a track that jumps out of a CD changer or a radio sequence gets attention — so labels pushed mastering engineers to raise average level. Since digital audio has a hard ceiling, the only way to get louder is to compress and limit the peaks, bringing everything closer to maximum.\n\nDo that far enough and the waveform stops looking like music and starts looking like a solid block. The transients that make a snare hit feel like an impact are the first thing to go, and the result is a record that sounds impressive for thirty seconds and tiring after ten minutes.\n\nSome nineties albums were remastered louder than their originals and are, by any technical measure, worse. A number of reissues have since been deliberately mastered quieter to undo it.\n\nStreaming has largely defused the incentive. Platforms normalise playback loudness, so a heavily compressed track gets turned down to match everything else and arrives sounding smaller rather than bigger. The arms race stopped paying, which is a rare case of a technical standard fixing an aesthetic problem.",["Some nineties reissues are quieter than their originals by design","Streaming loudness normalization has since reduced the incentive"],"mixer"),
("02-14","Ol' Dirty Bastard was born in Fort Greene","Hip-Hop","1968","Russell Jones grew up in Brooklyn and took his stage name from a kung-fu film, claiming there was 'no father to his style'. His off-key delivery was deliberate.\n\nThe name came from a martial arts film, and the explanation he gave for it — that there was no father to his style — is as good a description of the delivery as anyone has managed. He sang flat, drifted off the beat, shifted register mid-line, and did all of it on purpose, in a genre that at the time prized technical precision above almost everything.\n\nHe was cousins with RZA and GZA, and the three of them formed the core around which Wu-Tang Clan was built. On a record already full of distinctive voices, his was the one that could not be mistaken for anyone.\n\nThe recording process reflected the approach. Engineers have described takes captured in a single pass with no punch-ins, because the performances depended on momentum and fell apart if assembled from pieces.\n\nHis life was chaotic and publicly so — legal trouble, an escape from a court-ordered facility while a fugitive, and a period of genuine instability that the music press largely treated as entertainment.\n\nHe died in 2004, two days before his thirty-sixth birthday, of an accidental overdose. The eccentricity that made him famous was, in retrospect, quite clearly also an illness nobody treated.",["He was cousins with RZA and GZA of Wu-Tang","His vocal takes were often recorded in one pass with no punch-ins"],"mic"),
("02-15","Nat King Cole was a pianist first","Jazz","1943","Cole led an influential piano trio for years before singing became his main draw. He only sang because a club patron demanded it, by his own account.\n\nThe King Cole Trio — piano, guitar, and bass, with no drummer — was genuinely innovative. Removing the drums forced the guitar and bass to carry time, which produced a lighter, more conversational texture than the swing bands of the period and gave the piano an enormous amount of room. The format was widely copied, and Oscar Peterson among others has pointed to Cole's playing as a direct influence.\n\nHe sang reluctantly at first. The story he told involved a club patron demanding a vocal, though he later suggested the anecdote had been improved in the telling. Either way, the voice — light, precise, unforced — turned out to be the more commercial asset, and by the fifties the piano had become the secondary skill in the public's mind.\n\nHe was the first Black performer to host a national network variety show, in 1956. It ran roughly a year and was cancelled because no national sponsor would attach itself to it, which Cole described plainly at the time.\n\nHe smoked heavily, partly in the belief that it kept his voice in the register audiences knew, and died of lung cancer in 1965 at forty-five.",["His trio format dropped drums, unusual for the era","He was among the first Black artists to host a national TV show"],"piano"),
("02-16","Barclays Center changed which artists tour Brooklyn","Brooklyn","2012","Before 2012, most arena acts skipped Brooklyn entirely for Manhattan or the Meadowlands. The Atlantic Avenue arena reversed that overnight.\n\nThe touring map had been settled for decades. An arena-scale act playing New York played Madison Square Garden or crossed to New Jersey, and Brooklyn — two and a half million people, larger than most American cities — had no room at that scale at all. Audiences travelled to the show rather than the show travelling to them.\n\nBarclays changed the arithmetic by sitting on top of one of the busiest transit interchanges in the borough, where nine subway lines and the Long Island Rail Road converge. That location is most of the business case: an arena is only viable if a large number of people can reach it without driving.\n\nJay-Z, a minority investor at the time, played an eight-night opening run, which was both a marketing decision and a statement about whose borough it was.\n\nThe project was fiercely contested. It was part of the Atlantic Yards development, which used eminent domain to assemble the site and displaced residents and businesses, and the affordable housing promised alongside it arrived late and in smaller quantity than pledged.\n\nBoth things are true: it made Brooklyn a touring destination, and it cost people their homes to do it.",["Jay-Z played the opening eight-night run","It sits atop one of the busiest transit hubs in the borough"],"crowd"),
("02-17","Autotune was invented by an oil industry engineer","Pop","1997","Andy Hildebrand used seismic data math, designed to find oil underground, to detect and correct pitch. He built it as a subtle repair tool.\n\nHildebrand's previous work involved sending sound waves into the ground and analysing the reflections to map geological structures — a problem of extracting periodic signal from noise. Pitch detection in a human voice is mathematically the same problem, and he realised the autocorrelation techniques transferred directly.\n\nThe intended use was invisible repair: nudge a slightly flat note into tune so a good vocal take with one bad syllable does not have to be re-recorded. Used gently it is undetectable, and it has been used gently on a very large proportion of commercial records since.\n\nThe famous sound is a misuse. The plugin has a retune speed control, and setting it to its fastest value forces the voice to snap instantly between exact pitches rather than gliding, producing the stepped robotic effect. Hildebrand has said he included the extreme setting as a joke and never expected anyone to use it.\n\nCher's 'Believe' put it on the radio in 1998, the producers initially claimed it was a vocoder, and T-Pain later built an entire vocal identity on it.\n\nIt is now among the most consequential audio tools ever written, and it exists because someone was looking for oil.",["Cher's 'Believe' used the extreme setting the inventor called a joke","Hildebrand said he never expected anyone to use it that way"],"synth"),
("02-18","Yoko Ono and John Lennon recorded a heartbeat","Art Rock","1969","Their experimental albums included a recording of their unborn child's heartbeat, followed by silence after the pregnancy was lost. Few records have been that raw.\n\nThe piece appears on Unfinished Music No. 2: Life with the Lions, and it does exactly what it says — several minutes of a foetal heartbeat recorded in hospital, followed by silence marking the miscarriage. There is no arrangement, no commentary, and no attempt to make it bearable.\n\nContemporary reviews were largely hostile, and much of the hostility had less to do with the work than with Ono herself, who was blamed by a substantial part of the Beatles' audience for the band's dissolution and subjected to a level of racist and misogynist abuse that the music press of the time was comfortable printing.\n\nOno's background was in the Fluxus movement, which had been working with duration, silence, instruction pieces, and audience participation since the late fifties. The recordings that seemed inexplicable to rock critics were unremarkable within that tradition, and quite conservative by its standards.\n\nHer reputation has been substantially revised since. The sound-art practice of the following decades — field recording presented without mediation, duration as the primary material, refusal of conventional musical structure — runs directly through work like this.\n\nIt remains difficult to listen to, which was never accidental.",["Critics dismissed the work at the time","It anticipated decades of sound-art practice"],"tape"),
("02-19","Smokey Robinson wrote the song Motown almost rejected","Soul","1965","'The Tracks of My Tears' came from a guitar figure Marv Tarplin played for weeks before lyrics existed. Robinson wrote to the guitar, not the other way around.\n\nTarplin was the Miracles' guitarist and, by Robinson's own repeated account, an uncredited engine of the group's writing. He would work out a figure and play it over and over until something suggested itself, and several of the group's best-known songs were built that way — melody and words written to fit a guitar part that already existed.\n\nThe intro to 'The Tracks of My Tears' is exactly what Tarplin first improvised, kept intact. It functions as a hook before a single word is sung, which is unusual for the period and is a large part of why the record is instantly identifiable.\n\nRobinson's writing is the other half. The central image — a man whose smile is a performance, with the evidence of grief visible if you look closely enough — is the kind of controlling metaphor that most pop lyrics gesture at and abandon. He sustains it for the length of the song.\n\nBob Dylan is widely quoted as calling Robinson America's greatest living poet. Robinson has said he assumes Dylan was being generous, which is a fairly Smokey Robinson response.",["Bob Dylan reportedly called Robinson America's greatest living poet","The guitar intro was kept exactly as Tarplin first improvised it"],"guitar"),
("02-20","Kurt Cobain was born, and later hid melodies in noise","Grunge","1967","Cobain's writing method paired sweet pop melody with deliberately abrasive tone, a tension he credited to loving both the Beatles and Black Flag.\n\nThe structural signature is the quiet-loud-quiet dynamic, which Nirvana took fairly directly from the Pixies — Cobain said as much, repeatedly and without embarrassment. A verse played clean and restrained, a chorus that arrives at full distortion, and the contrast doing the emotional work that an arrangement would otherwise have to build gradually.\n\nUnderneath the noise, the songs are conventionally melodic. Strip the distortion from most of Nevermind and what remains is pop writing in a fairly traditional mould, which is precisely why it crossed over so completely.\n\nCobain disliked the album's sound. Butch Vig produced and Andy Wallace mixed it into something considerably more polished than the band had wanted, and Cobain complained publicly about the gloss almost immediately — In Utero, with Steve Albini, was the corrective.\n\nHis lyrics were frequently written minutes before the vocal take, assembled from notebooks rather than composed as arguments, which is why they resist interpretation and why interpreting them anyway became a cottage industry.\n\nHe died by suicide in April 1994, at twenty-seven. If this is a difficult subject for you personally, it is worth talking to someone you trust.",["'Nevermind' was mixed to sound more polished than the band wanted","He wrote lyrics minutes before recording many vocals"],"guitar"),
("02-21","Nina Simone played Carnegie Hall, but not as she planned","Jazz","1963","When Simone finally reached Carnegie Hall, she noted bitterly that she arrived as a pop singer, not the classical concert pianist she trained to be.\n\nThe ambition had been specific and lifelong. She trained from childhood toward a career as a concert pianist, her hometown raised money for her lessons, and she believed until her death that her rejection by the Curtis Institute was racial rather than musical. Reaching Carnegie Hall on somebody else's terms was not the vindication it looked like from outside.\n\nShe made the point on stage. Opening a set with a Bach prelude in front of an audience expecting popular song was a deliberate act of insistence about what she actually was, and her improvisations move through contrapuntal writing in a way no other performer in that context was attempting.\n\nThe same period pushed her decisively into civil rights work. 'Mississippi Goddam' was written in 1963 after the murder of Medgar Evers and the Birmingham church bombing, and it is furious in a way almost nothing on American radio was permitted to be.\n\nThe cost was concrete. Stations refused to play the record, some returned promotional copies broken, and bookings dried up. She was entirely clear-eyed that the position she took was expensive, and she took it anyway.",["She opened sets with Bach preludes to make the point","Her civil-rights songs cost her radio play and income"],"piano"),
("02-22","Flatbush's Caribbean records shaped Brooklyn radio","Brooklyn","1970","Waves of Caribbean migration made Flatbush a hub for calypso, soca and reggae pressings. Local record shops imported Jamaican dubplates before mainstream stores carried them.\n\nThe migration followed the 1965 Immigration and Nationality Act, which removed the national-origin quotas that had effectively barred large-scale Caribbean immigration for four decades. Central Brooklyn — Flatbush, East Flatbush, Crown Heights — became home to one of the largest Caribbean populations outside the islands, and it still is.\n\nThe music arrived with the people, and it arrived through shops rather than radio. Small record stores imported pressings directly from Kingston and Port of Spain, often carrying dubplates and singles months before anything reached mainstream American retail. If you wanted to know what was current in Jamaica, the fastest route in New York was a Flatbush counter.\n\nSound systems set up in backyards, church halls, and basements, running the same competitive model as Kingston.\n\nThe connection to hip-hop is direct rather than atmospheric. DJ Kool Herc was born in Kingston and brought sound system practice — the enormous rig, the toasting, the focus on the break — to the Bronx, and the form that grew out of it is unimaginable without that transfer.\n\nWalk Church Avenue on a summer Saturday and the transmission is still audible in real time.",["Sound systems set up in backyards and church halls across the neighborhood","That circuit fed directly into New York hip-hop's early sound"],"radio"),
("02-23","George Frideric Handel went blind writing music","Classical","1751","Handel continued composing and performing after losing his sight, dictating revisions from memory. Audiences reported he still improvised organ solos flawlessly.\n\nThe blindness came on gradually from around 1751, during work on the oratorio Jephtha, and he noted the difficulty in the manuscript itself. Several operations by the surgeon John Taylor — a travelling oculist of dubious competence who also operated on Bach, with equally poor results — made things worse rather than better.\n\nHe kept working. Revisions were dictated to assistants, and he continued to appear publicly at the organ, where his improvisation had always been a substantial part of the draw. Accounts from the period describe the playing as undiminished, which is plausible: keyboard improvisation is largely a matter of internalised pattern and hearing, and sight contributes less to it than one might assume.\n\nBy this point he had been in London for forty years and had shifted his entire business model from Italian opera to English oratorio, which turned out to be both artistically productive and commercially necessary.\n\nHe died in 1759 and was buried in Westminster Abbey with a reported three thousand mourners — an extraordinary turnout for a composer, and an indication of how thoroughly a German-born musician had become a British institution.",["He was buried in Westminster Abbey with 3,000 mourners","His late oratorios were largely written without being able to see the page"],"strings"),
("02-24","The Sugarhill Gang were not a real group","Hip-Hop","1979","Label owner Sylvia Robinson assembled three unknowns after hearing rap at a party. One was auditioned while working at a pizzeria.\n\nRobinson had a long career behind her — a hit as half of Mickey & Sylvia, then a label owner — and she recognised a commercial opportunity that the people actually making the music had not yet monetised. Rap existed in parks, community centres, and clubs across the Bronx and Harlem, and none of it was on record.\n\nRather than sign an established crew, she assembled a group. Big Bank Hank was working at a pizzeria in New Jersey when he was auditioned, and the three members had no standing in the scene whatsoever.\n\nThe part that still rankles is the rhymes. Hank's verse draws on lyrics by Grandmaster Caz of the Cold Crush Brothers, who by most accounts lent him a notebook and received no credit and no royalties. One of the most commercially significant records in the genre's history contains uncredited work by a man who never saw a penny from it.\n\nThat set a pattern the industry repeated for years: outsiders capturing the commercial value of a form while the people who built it watched. The record opened the door, and a lot of the people who had been standing at it were not the ones who walked through.",["Big Bank Hank reportedly borrowed rhymes from Grandmaster Caz","Caz received no credit or royalties for lines used on the record"],"turntable"),
("02-25","George Harrison's sitar lesson changed rock","Rock","1966","Harrison studied under Ravi Shankar, who insisted he learn properly rather than use the instrument as decoration. Indian modes then filtered into Western pop.\n\nShankar was a serious classical musician with no particular interest in Western pop and, by his own account, no clear idea who the Beatles were when they were introduced. His condition for teaching was that Harrison approach the instrument as a discipline — sitting properly, learning scales and rhythmic cycles — rather than treating it as an exotic sound to layer onto a rock record.\n\nHarrison had already used sitar decoratively on 'Norwegian Wood', which he later described as fairly rudimentary. The study that followed produced something different: 'Within You Without You' is built on Indian classical structure, features no other Beatle, and uses tabla and dilruba players rather than the band.\n\nThe wider consequence was a vocabulary shift. Drones, modal scales that do not resolve the way Western harmony does, and long non-developmental forms entered rock and stayed, feeding directly into psychedelia and later into minimalism's popular reception.\n\nIt also opened a long and complicated conversation about appropriation that has not closed. Shankar himself was ambivalent, welcoming the attention to Indian music and frequently exasperated by audiences who treated a centuries-old tradition as a lifestyle accessory.",["Shankar initially had no idea who the Beatles were","'Within You Without You' features no other Beatle"],"strings"),
("02-26","Fats Domino sold more records than anyone but Elvis","R&B","1955","The New Orleans pianist's rolling triplets defined early rock piano. He recorded almost everything at Cosimo Matassa's tiny J&M studio.\n\nThe triplet figure — the right hand rolling three notes to the beat against a steady backbeat — became the default vocabulary for rock and roll piano, and it descends from New Orleans barrelhouse and boogie traditions that predate the term rock and roll by decades. Domino did not invent it. He made it the standard.\n\nJ&M Recording was in the back of an appliance shop on Rampart Street, and it was small enough that the room itself shaped the sound: a natural slapback, limited separation, and a house band of extraordinary New Orleans musicians who played on an enormous share of the era's records. Little Richard, Lloyd Price, and Ray Charles all cut there.\n\nDomino's commercial position in the fifties is frequently understated. He sold more records than any rock and roll artist of the decade other than Elvis Presley, and he did it while touring a segregated country under conditions that put him in genuine danger.\n\nHe refused to leave New Orleans after Hurricane Katrina in 2005 and was initially reported missing before being rescued from his flooded Lower Ninth Ward home. He died there in 2017.",["The studio was in the back of an appliance shop","Its cramped room created the signature slapback sound"],"piano"),
("02-27","Brooklyn's Prospect Park bandshell started a free summer tradition","Brooklyn","1979","Celebrate Brooklyn began as a modest neighborhood series and grew into one of America's longest-running free outdoor festivals, minutes from Ditmas Park.\n\nThe founding context matters. New York in the late seventies was near-bankrupt, park maintenance had collapsed, and Prospect Park had a reputation for danger that kept a lot of people out of it. Programming free concerts at the bandshell was partly cultural and partly a deliberate effort to give the surrounding neighbourhoods a reason to walk back into the park after dark.\n\nIt worked, slowly, and the series has now run for more than four decades — among the longest-running free outdoor performance festivals in the country. The booking has stayed unusually broad: Afrobeat, salsa, indie rock, jazz, classical, film screenings with live scores, and a steady presence of Brooklyn artists alongside international names.\n\nThe bandshell sits at the Prospect Park West and Ninth Street corner, walkable from Ditmas Park, Flatbush, Windsor Terrace, and Park Slope, which is most of why it functions as a genuinely shared event rather than one neighbourhood's.\n\nEntry remains free for the majority of the programme, with a small number of benefit shows ticketed to fund the rest. That model is the whole argument: the thing works because nobody has to decide whether it is worth the money.",["It launched partly to bring people back into the park after a rough decade","Acts range from Afrobeat legends to local Brooklyn bands"],"crowd"),
("02-28","The Moog synthesizer needed a hit to be taken seriously","Electronic","1968","Wendy Carlos's 'Switched-On Bach' proved the Moog could make music, not just noise. It became one of the best-selling classical records ever.\n\nThe instrument had been available for a few years and was largely regarded as a laboratory curiosity or a source of sound effects. Robert Moog's modular system was difficult to use, unstable in tuning, and had no obvious repertoire, and the electronic music being made with comparable equipment was largely academic and forbidding.\n\nCarlos's choice of Bach was strategic. Bach's writing is contrapuntal, precisely notated, and instrumentally neutral — it has been transcribed for every ensemble imaginable for two centuries — so realising it electronically was a demonstration rather than a provocation.\n\nThe labour involved was enormous. The Moog was monophonic, playing one note at a time, so every voice in a fugue had to be recorded separately and layered, with tuning drifting between passes and requiring constant correction. Carlos worked with Rachel Elkind on the production across many months.\n\nIt sold in the hundreds of thousands and became one of the best-selling classical records ever released, which put the synthesizer in front of musicians who had dismissed it.\n\nCarlos went on to score A Clockwork Orange and Tron, and is a trans woman who came out publicly in 1979, at considerable personal cost.",["Each note had to be played and layered one at a time","The instrument was monophonic, so chords required many passes"],"synth"),
("02-29","Leap day gave Gioachino Rossini a rare birthday","Classical","1792","Born on 29 February, Rossini joked he only aged once every four years. He retired from opera at 37 and spent decades writing small pieces and cooking.\n\nThe retirement is the genuinely strange part. Rossini was the most successful opera composer in Europe, had written roughly forty operas by his late thirties, and then stopped almost entirely after William Tell in 1829, living another thirty-nine years without producing another one. Explanations have included illness, exhaustion, wealth, and simple disinclination, and none of them fully account for it.\n\nHe wrote fast when he was writing. The Barber of Seville was completed in something like three weeks, and he was notorious for reusing overtures between operas when the deadline demanded it — the Barber's overture had already served two previous works.\n\nWhat he did instead was cook and write small pieces. He was a serious gastronome, several dishes carry his name, and the late piano and vocal miniatures he produced for private performance were collected under the title Sins of Old Age. Several are named after food.\n\nHe died in 1868 and left the bulk of a substantial fortune to endow a music conservatory in his home town of Pesaro, which still operates.\n\nBy his own count he had lived about nineteen birthdays.",["He wrote 'The Barber of Seville' in about three weeks","He named several compositions after food"],"strings"),
]
